                <div class="border">
                    <h2>Terminology</h2>
                    <p>
                        When discussing how efficient or "good" a Sorting Algorithm is, 
                        we often look at a handful of characteristics: 
                        <b>Time Complexity</b>, 
                        <b>Space Efficiency</b>, 
                        <b>Stability</b>, 
                        and <b>In-place</b>.

                        <!-- Time Efficiency -->
                        <h3>Time Complexity</h3>
                        Time Complexity, also called Time Efficiency, measures how long an algorithm takes to sort a list.
                        <br>
                        We use the notation <b>n</b> when measuring Time Complexity. If we had a list of five numbers, <b>n</b> would be 5.
                        <br>
                        Then, in order from best to worst, we typically use the following to measure Time Complexity:
                        <ol>
                            <li><b>1</b>: Always takes the same amount of time, regardless of <b>n</b>. This would be a single operation, like reading the value of a certain number from the list.</li>
                            <li><b>logn</b>: Scales logarithmically with <b>n</b>.</li>
                            <li><b>n</b>: Scales linearly with <b>n</b>.</li>
                            <li><b>n logn</b>: Scales in between linearly and quadratically.</li>
                            <li><b>n<sup>2</sup></b>: Scales quadratically with <b>n</b>.</li>
                            <li><b>n<sup>3</sup></b>: Scales cubically with <b>n</b>.</li>
                            <li><b>2<sup>n</sup></b>: Scales exponentially with <b>n</b>.</li>
                            <li><b>n!</b>: Scales factorially with <b>n</b>.</li>
                        </ol>
                        So, if we have a list of 5 numbers, and we used an algorithm with a Time Complexity of <b>n<sup>2</sup></b>, 
                        then our final Time Complexity would be 25.
                        <br>
                        <br>
                        We cannot use Time Complexity to exactly predict how long it will take to sort a list.
                        This is because the actual time varies based on different hardware, how quickly a computer is operating at that moment,
                        and what other work the computer may be doing at the same time.
                        <br>
                        Instead, we use Time Complexity to say generally how quickly an algorithm will work,
                        especially when compared to other algorithms.

                        <!-- Space Efficiency -->
                        <h3>Space Complexity</h3>
                        Space Complexity measures how much additional memory an algorithm uses when sorting.
                        <br>
                        It uses the same notation as Time Complexity.
                        <br>
                        <br>
                        During the early development of computers memory was very limited, 
                        so it may have been necessary to choose an algorithm with better Space Complexity over better Time Complexity.
                        Today this matters less for smaller lists, but this can still be relevant for large lists of information.

                        <!-- Stable -->
                        <h3>Stability</h3>
                        An algorithm is considered stable if all elements with the same value are in the same order after being sorted.
                        Conversely, an algorithm is considered unstable if not all elements with the same value are in the same order afterwards.
                        <br>
                        <br>
                        For example, take the list: [3, 1, <i>3</i>, 4, <b>3</b>, 2]. Notice how there are three 3s, one italic, one bold, and one regular.
                        <br>
                        A stable algorithm would sort the list to: [1, 2, 3, <i>3</i>, <b>3</b>, 4]. The threes are in the same order as before.
                        <br>
                        An unstable algorithm would sort the list to: [1, 2, <b>3</b>, 3, <i>3</i>, 4]. Now the threes are in a different order.
                        <br>
                        <br>
                        Stability matters when dealing with several types of data.
                        <br>
                        Lets say that you wanted to sort a list of countries by continent and name.
                        We would have to sort the list twice, first by name, then by continent. 
                        If we used an unstable algorithm, the names may no longer be sorted by name after sorting them by continent.
                        We would need a stable algorithm to be able to sort the list in multiple ways.

                        <!-- In-Place -->
                        <h3>In-Place</h3>
                        <p>
                            An in-place algorithm writes directly to the array while it is sorting,
                            while an out-of-place algorithm first writes to a secondary array, and later writes to the main array.
                            <br><br>
                            Whether an algorithm is in-place or not typically correlates with its Space Complexity,
                            with an in-place algorithm often having a lower Space Complexity than an out-of-place one.
                        </p>
                    </p>
