                    <!-- Heading -->
                    <h2>Asynchronous Sorts</h2>

                    <!-- Descripion -->
                    <p>
                        Many algorithms run synchronously, 
                        meaning a computer could not perform seperate sections at the same time.
                        <br>
                        Imagine a group of people where each is trying to find the smallest number in a list.
                        At best they would find the same number as the smallest, and at worst they might cause one another to make mistakes.
                        <br>
                        <br>
                        However, some algorithms lend themselves to having different sections sorted at the same time.
                        Imagine if two people split the list into two halves, then sorted each half independently.
                        In this case they would not risk messing each other up, because they are working on different sections.
                        <br>
                        <br>
                        Each of the algorithms listed here can be ran asynchronously. 
                        Each is a variation of an algorithm that runs asynchronously.
                        <br>
                        <br>
                        Note: This page uses JavaScript, which cannot truly run asynchronous code. 
                        The performance of these algorithms may not reflect how their real-life performance,
                        but the visualization will give you an idea of how they behave.
                    </p>                    

